# resolution_tripwire - KNOWN-WRONG BEHAVIOR, PINNED SO IT CANNOT MOVE
#                       SILENTLY.  Read tests/tripwire_test.cryo first.
#
# WHAT IS STILL OPEN HERE (spec §5.1): a bare name resolves with NOTHING in
# scope. `ResolutionTripwire::Orphan` has no `import` at all and names a type
# only `Depot` declares; it resolves anyway, via the global leaf index. Both
# `WRONG_` tests pin that. When it is fixed they must become E0203 -- follow
# the flip protocol below.
#
# THE FLIP PROTOCOL. When a fix lands, the matching test STARTS FAILING. That
# is the design. Move the case to a `compile_fail` project with the right
# `expect.diagnostic` in the SAME change that makes it an error -- do not
# adjust the assertion to get back to green.
#
# THE PROTOCOL HAS BEEN EXERCISED ONCE, ON THIS FILE (2026-08-05). The
# visibility half -- "a `private` function is callable from another module" --
# left for tests/tests/projects/visibility_gate, which asserts E0353 through
# each of the gate's three binding doors. Only the public CONTROL stayed, in
# tests/vault.cryo, because it has to keep running rather than merely
# compiling.
#
# THE HISTORY IS WORTH KEEPING, because the same trap is still live for the
# §5.1 half. The visibility gate was built and REVERTED on 2026-08-04: all
# three doors were shut and the compiler and stdlib self-built clean, yet the
# test tree failed with 8 errors on `std::fmt::display::fmt_err` -- a private
# function whose every caller is in its own file. Every gate needs a "does the
# use site live in the declaring module?" exemption, and that one asked
# `current_module_ns()`, the AMBIENT CURSOR, which points at whatever module
# is being processed. The f-string lowering synthesizes those `fmt_err` calls
# and they were checked with the cursor parked in `std::core::result`.
#
# It was unblocked by giving the exemption real provenance
# (`module_ns_sym_of_file(span.file)` -- the module that WROTE the syntax).
# Measured on examples/09-json-config: 204 of 204 "owner not in scope" events
# were that same false positive, and all 204 vanished.
#
# THE COUNTER LESSON GENERALIZES AND IS NOT RETIRED. `FnBindPrivateBound == 0`
# read like evidence the gate was safe to switch on. It was not: it counts
# what a gate would CATCH, and the failure mode was what it would BREAK. Any
# fix to the §5.1 half above will have the same shape.
#
# A sibling defect - a path silently dropping TRAILING module segments - was
# fixed on 2026-08-04 and is pinned at
# tests/tests/negative/E0203_path_drops_trailing_segment.cryo.

